INT. DUSTY RECORDS - STUDIO B - DAY

A converted barn in the high desert. Egg-crate foam stapled to the walls. A tumbleweed, a real one, sits in the corner like a joke nobody got around to cleaning up.

Clementine, 30s, boots up on the console, picks at a lap steel. Abel, 40s, the producer, flannel and tired eyes, stares at the monitor. Hux, 20s, leans in the doorway chewing a toothpick. A teenage kid in an apron sweeps the same square foot of floor over and over. A dog snoozes under the couch.

CLOSE ON the tape machine, reels turning.

ABEL
that's not what we cut yesterday.

CLEMENTINE
it's close.

ABEL
it's not close. you slowed the bridge.

HUX
she didn't slow it. i did.

ABEL
why'd you slow it?

HUX
i dunno. felt right. felt like, you know. like riding in.

CLEMENTINE
riding in where.

HUX
town. i dunno. i said i dunno.

ANGLE ON the glass. THE BAND, five men in dusty hats, stand in the live room staring at the control room, waiting. One of them lifts a fiddle bow and lowers it again.

THE BAND
(in unison, muffled through glass)
we ready?

ABEL
(pressing the talkback)
not yet.

CLEMENTINE
you always say not yet.

ABEL
because it's never yet.

HUX
(quietly)
you could just say yes, Abel. once.

ABEL
i don't say yes to things that aren't finished.

CLEMENTINE
nothing's ever finished. that's the whole thing about it.

The kid stops sweeping. The dog lifts its head. Abel stands, grabs his hat, crosses to the door. Hux doesn't move out of his way.

HUX
where you going?

ABEL
out. air.

CLEMENTINE
you leave, the take's gone.

ABEL
then it's gone.

He shoulders past Hux, pushes the door. Heat and sunlight pour in. He stops. WE SEE the red ON AIR light above the door, still lit.

BACK TO SCENE: Clementine's hand is on the record button. She hasn't taken it off. She looks at him through the open door, and doesn't say anything.

THE BAND
(through the glass, hopeful)
we ready now?

ABEL
(without turning)
yeah.
